---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// Bump this to the date you actually re-verified the rows below (Step 2a).
const checked = '2026-08-31';

// Three states, not two. Rectangle's paid tier has custom sizes but no grid,
// and flattening that to "no" understates it — the same capability is why
// Divvy's and Moom's cells read yes.
const yes = { mark: '●', label: 'Yes' };
const no = { mark: '○', label: 'No' };
const pro = { mark: 'Pro', label: 'In the paid tier only' };

const rows = [
  { name: 'macOS built-in tiling', href: null,
    grid: no, regions: no, rebind: no, price: 'Free' },
  { name: 'Orthant', href: null, self: true,
    grid: yes, regions: yes, rebind: yes, price: 'Free' },
  { name: 'Rectangle', href: 'https://rectangleapp.com',
    grid: no, regions: pro, rebind: yes, price: 'Free · paid Pro tier' },
  { name: 'Divvy', href: 'https://mizage.com/divvy/',
    grid: yes, regions: yes, rebind: yes, price: '$13.99' },
  { name: 'Magnet', href: 'https://magnet.crowdcafe.com',
    grid: no, regions: no, rebind: yes, price: '$4.99' },
  { name: 'Moom', href: 'https://manytricks.com/moom/',
    grid: yes, regions: yes, rebind: yes, price: '$15' },
];
---
<Base
  title="How Orthant compares · Orthant"
  description="macOS already tiles windows. What a grid-based window manager adds on top, and how Orthant compares to Rectangle, Divvy, Magnet and Moom."
  path="/compare/"
>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h1>How Orthant compares</h1>

    <h2>macOS already does halves and quarters. Why add anything?</h2>
    <p>
      Since Sequoia, macOS tiles windows in the box: halves, quarters, and drag-to-edge. For a
      lot of people that is genuinely enough, and if it is enough for you, you do not need
      Orthant.
    </p>
    <p>What it does not do:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No grid.</strong> You get the fractions Apple chose. There is no way to say
        "left two thirds" or "the middle column of five".</li>
      <li><strong>No custom regions.</strong> Nothing to name, save, or bind.</li>
      <li><strong>No rebinding.</strong> The shortcuts are fixed, and they hang off the Globe
        key.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Orthant adds those three things. Everything else about how macOS behaves is left alone, and
      the two coexist.
    </p>

    <h2>Against other window managers</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>App</th><th>Grid overlay</th><th>Custom regions</th><th>Rebindable</th><th>Price</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map((r) => (
          <tr class={r.self ? 'self' : undefined}>
            <td>{r.href ? <a href={r.href} rel="noopener">{r.name}</a> : r.name}</td>
            {[r.grid, r.regions, r.rebind].map((c) => (
              <td><span aria-hidden="true">{c.mark}</span><span class="sr">{c.label}</span></td>
            ))}
            <td>{r.price}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p class="quiet">Prices and capabilities checked {checked}. Corrections welcome: <a
      href="https://github.com/orthant-app/orthant/issues">open an issue</a>. Magnet's own site
      lists no price; the figure above is from its Mac App Store listing.</p>

    <h2>Which one should you use?</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Rectangle is excellent, free, and widely used.</strong> If you want reliable
      halves and quarters on keyboard shortcuts, use it. This is not a pitch to switch.
    </p>
    <p>
      Orthant is for the case Rectangle's free tier does not cover: when you want to{' '}
      <em>draw</em> where a window goes on a grid, and keep the result as a named shortcut.
      Rectangle <strong>Pro</strong> can define a custom size and position too. What it does
      not have is a grid to draw one on.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Divvy and Moom cover that ground too</strong>, as the table says. Both are
      long-standing, well-made apps, and if you already own one you do not need Orthant. The
      difference there is not capability. It is that Orthant is free and open source, and they
      are paid.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Table chrome comes from the shared `.prose table` rules in tokens.css —
     the docs tables and this one had drifted into two different looks. What
     stays here is only what is specific to this table. */
  table { min-width: 480px; }
  td:first-child, th:first-child { padding-left: 12px; }
  tr.self { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 6%, transparent); }
  .quiet { color: var(--label-secondary); font-size: 15px; }
  /* The capability glyphs are decorative; the real value is the adjacent text,
     which is the only thing a screen reader gets on the page most dependent on
     being read accurately. */
  .sr { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip-path: inset(50%); white-space: nowrap; }
</style>
